{
  "id": "grammar.a2.01.01.participios",
  "title": "Talking About What You've Done",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "The pretérito perfecto is formed with the present tense of haber + a past participle. For regular verbs, -ar verbs normally change to -ado, while -er and -ir verbs change to -ido. Use the tense to talk about recent experiences or completed actions that are relevant to the present. The auxiliary changes for the subject; the participle does not change."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "title": "Haber (Present Tense)",
      "rows": [
        [
          "yo",
          "he"
        ],
        [
          "tú",
          "has"
        ],
        [
          "él / ella / usted",
          "ha"
        ],
        [
          "nosotros / nosotras",
          "hemos"
        ],
        [
          "ellos / ellas / ustedes",
          "han"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "spanish": "He viajado a Vietnam.",
          "english": "I have travelled to Vietnam."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Hemos llegado al hotel.",
          "english": "We have arrived at the hotel."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Han reservado una habitación.",
          "english": "They have booked a room."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "¿Has hablado con la recepcionista?",
          "english": "Have you spoken to the receptionist?"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "title": "Regular Participle Endings",
      "rows": [
        [
          "hablado",
          "spoken"
        ],
        [
          "comido",
          "eaten"
        ],
        [
          "vivido",
          "lived"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "tip",
      "content": "Do not change the participle for the subject: he *viajado*, *hemos* viajado, *han* viajado."
    },
    {
